
  Next question, and it's the one thing here that's genuinely expensive to change later: [1mwhat value gets stored as the [0m
  [1midentity?[0m

  Everything else in this design is internal and swappable. The stored shape is not — once other forms are stamping
  submissions with it, changing it means touching every consumer and invalidating anything already written.

  Right now the only identity in the codebase is the username the user typed. [38;5;153mlogin[39m never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m; it's a
  stub returning [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m. So there is no server-issued id available unless we build that call
  as part of this.

  - [1mUsername only[0m — store the string. Simplest possible thing, matches "keep it simple" exactly. Costs: when the real
    API lands and returns an actual user id, every consumer that reads a username has to change.
  - [1mSmall record, id defaults to username[0m — store [38;5;153m{ id, username }[39m where [38;5;153mid[39m is the username today and becomes the
    server's id later. Costs one extra field and one line. Buys: consumers read [38;5;153m.id[39m from day one, so the API landing is
    a change inside the store and nowhere else.
  - [1mReal server id now[0m — implement the actual [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m call so login returns a true id. Costs: this stops being a
    small task; you'd be building error handling, network failure states, and probably a mock server for tests.

  [1mI'd recommend the middle one.[0m It's one field of insurance on the only decision here that's costly to reverse, and it
  doesn't pull the API work into scope. If you'd rather not carry a field you don't use yet, the first option is
  defensible — just know the consumers are what pay for it later.
